  "updated": "2026-09-03T19:52:53.720245+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStrong 594 dam ridging remains centered across the Southeast \nwith the axis of the ridge extended north through the Plains. \nLocally, we're parked beneath northwest mid-level flow, and \nsubtle impulses in the flow combined with mesoscale surface \nfeatures will continue to be the driver of our thunderstorm \ncomplexes over the next 24-36 hours.\n\nLingering showers and debris cloud coverage from Thursday morning's \nconvection will clear out by early afternoon, and satellite imagery \nshows clear skies arriving in the wake. This should allow for quick, \nefficient heating and diurnal cu development while the atmosphere \nrecovers. What we then turn our attention to is a developing \nthunderstorm complex over lower Michigan that 12z CAMs all dive \nsoutheast and into northwest PA after 3-4pm. Some suggest that there \ncould be a few discrete cells popping out ahead of the complex in \nwestern PA around that same time. These should have a rather potent \nenvironment to work with as exploration of point soundings \nacross the area shows 2500-3000 J/kg of MLCAPE, 35-40 knots of \neffective shear, and steep mid-levle lapse rates exceeding 7 \nC/km. One difference from Wednesday is that there appears to be \na bit less mid- level dry air which could support better storm \norganization and intensity today. All hazards will be on the \ntable with both modes; initial discrete cells could become \nsupercellular and pose a wind, hail, and tornado threat, \nespecially if dew points don't mix out in time and we're left \nwith low to mid 70s as noted by the usually drier HRRR. Damaging\nwind will be the primary threat with the more linear mode, but \ncan't ignore 0-3 km shear values nearing 30 knots and the \npotential existing for a quick QLCS tornado spin up. Again, \nthink most likely timing is into NW PA around 4-5pm and \nPittsburgh metro around 6-7pm. A localized Enhanced Risk has \nbeen introduced for the heightened wind threat from Pittsburgh \nnorth. In addition, with repeated rounds of rain the past 24-36 \nhours, flash flooding will also pose a threat as flash flood \nguidance values along I-80 sit around 1-1.25\" in one hour. That \nmay be achieved rather easily this afternoon with a storm or two\nin the same area. For that reason, have issued a Flood Watch \nthrough tonight north of Pittsburgh.\n\nCome Friday, some CAMs suggest another early morning push of showers \nand storms albeit with less intensity. Finer details on that should \narise later today when we see the initiation of it off to our \nnorthwest. Later Friday, we'll yet again face another MCS threat \nwith a cluster developing way off to our north in Minnesota and \ndiving its way through the Great Lakes into our neck of the woods. \nExact track and timing of it is even more uncertain as its several \ndomino pieces away from where were at currently, and morning \nconvection will have a say in afternoon intensity as well. The front \nitself should then pass through during the late afternoon on Friday \nushering in a drier airmass as surface high pressure arrives from \nthe north, though many of the 12z CAMs now think we have yet another \ncar in the MCS train riding the southeastern periphery of the \nsurface high and perhaps impacting us again overnight Friday night. \nNot putting a ton of stock in this possibility yet, but it's \nintriguing that a couple models are coming to a similar solution.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAside from the storms, unseasonably warm conditions continue \nthrough Thursday. Heat risk will remain in the major category \nwith pockets of extreme in the valleys and urban areas where \ntemperatures reach the low to mid 90s and dew points in the 70s \nresulting in heat indices exceeding 100F. Some localized cooler \nareas are possible underneath increased cloud cover form early \nmorning storms, but still expect that we'll be able to recover \nand heat well.",
